To calculate the standard enthalpy change (ΔH) for the reaction, we can use the formula:

ΔH = Σ(n * ΔHf) (products) - Σ(n * ΔHf) (reactants)

where n is the stoichiometric coefficient of each reactant or product in the balanced equation, and ΔHf is the standard enthalpy of formation of each substance.

In this case, the balanced equation is:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The standard enthalpies of formation are:

ΔHf(CaO(s)) = -635.1 kJ/mol
ΔHf(H2O(l)) = -285.8 kJ/mol
ΔHf(Ca(OH)2(s)) = -986.2 kJ/mol

Now, we can plug these values into the formula:

ΔH = (1 * (-986.2 kJ/mol)) - (1 * (-635.1 kJ/mol) + 1 * (-285.8 kJ/mol))

ΔH = -986.2 kJ/mol - (-635.1 kJ/mol - 285.8 kJ/mol)

ΔH = -986.2 kJ/mol + 635.1 kJ/mol + 285.8 kJ/mol

ΔH = -986.2 + 635.1 + 220.1

ΔH = -100.1 kJ/mol

