","To calculate the power output of the galvanic cell, we first need to find the cell potential (Ecell) using the Nernst equation, then the current (I) and then the power (P).

### Step 1: Calculate the Cell Potential (Ecell)
The half-reactions are:
- Anode (cathode):  
\[
\text{H}_2(g) + 2e^- \rightarrow 2H^+(aq) \quad \text{Ecell,} \text{anode} = +0.0 \, \text{V}
\]
- Anode (anode):  
\[
\text{Cu}_2(s) + 2e^- \rightarrow 2Cl^2(s) \quad \text{Ecell,} \text{catode} = +0.34 \, \text{V}
\]

The overall reaction is:
\[
\text{H}_2(g) + \text{Cl}_2(s) \rightarrow 2H^2(aq) + \text{Cu}_2(s)
\]

The cell potential at standard conditions (1 atm pressure, 1 M concentration) is:
\[
E_{\text{cell}} = E_{\text{anode}} - E_{\text{catode}} = 0.34 \, \text{V} - 0.0 \, \text{V} = 0.34 \, \text{V}
\]

The Nernst equation is:
\[
E_{\text{cell}} = E_{\text{cell}}^{\text{cell}} - \frac{RT}{F} \ln K
\]
For a reaction involving hydrogen and copper, the Nernst equation is:
\[
E_{\text{cell}} = E_{\text{cell}}^{\text{std}} - \frac{RT}{F} \ln \left( \frac{1}{1} \right
\]

