","Let the cube has side length $2$, so its vertices are $(0,0,0)$ and $(2,2,2)$. The center of the cube is $(1,1,1)$. The center of the central sphere is at the center of the cube, so its coordinates are $(1,1,1)$. Let the radius of the spheres is $r$. The center of the central sphere is at $(1,1,1)$, so its volume is a sphere with radius $r$ centered at $(1,1,1)$.

The sphere touching three faces of the cube has its center at $(r,r,r)$. Since it is tangent to the cube, its volume is a sphere with radius $r$ centered at $(r,r,r)$. Since it is tangent to the central sphere, the distance from $(1,1,1)$ to $(r,r,r)$ is $2r$. The distance between these two points is:
\[
\sqrt{(1-r)^2 + (1-r)^2 + (1-r)^2} = 2r \implies \sqrt{3(1-r)^2} = 2r \implies \sqrt{3}|1-r| = 2r.
\]
