A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by Newton's second law: \(F = ma\). The force acting on the proton is due to the electric field: \(F = qE\), where \(q\) is the charge of the proton and \(E\) is the electric field strength. Substituting \(F = qE\) into \(F = ma\), we get \(qE = ma\), so \(a = \frac{qE}{m}\).  
Substituting the given values:  
\[
a = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.76 \times 10^{8} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
The magnitude of the acceleration of the proton is \(\boxed{4.76 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  

(b) The acceleration is constant, so we can use the kinematic equation \(v = v_0 + at\), where \(v\) is the final velocity, \(v_0\) is the initial velocity, \(a\) is the acceleration, and \(t\) is the time. Since the proton starts from rest, \(v_0 = 0\). Solving for \(t\):  
\[
t = \frac{v}{a} = \frac{30000 \, \text{m/s}}{4.75 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2} = 6.35 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}
